WORKSPACE PLANNING FOR TWO NEW WORKSPACES
=========================================
Time : 2026-10-14 22:00

WHAT WAS FOUND
  E: now holds three workspaces: E:\deepseek (the established one, 2013 files), plus two new EMPTY
  folders the user created: E:\Deepseek_kaka and E:\Deepseek_kolp.

PREREQUISITE FIX (had to come first)
  The tools scripts hard-coded $Root = 'E:\Deepseek'. Copying one into a new workspace would have made
  it create conversation folders back in the OLD workspace. All four tools scripts now derive the root
  from their own location:
      if (-not $Root) { $Root = Split-Path -Parent $PSScriptRoot }
  An explicit -Root still overrides, so behaviour in E:\deepseek is unchanged. Verified by running the
  copied script inside E:\Deepseek_kaka: it created conv-* under kaka in all six categories while
  E:\deepseek\deliverables stayed at 5 entries. The test folders were then removed.
  While doing this I also found and repaired real corruption: the "rebuilt" header comment of three
  scripts had been written through Node with the Chinese text mangled into question marks - the
  PowerShell-to-Node stdin hazard already documented in rule 0. Rewritten with a BOM-aware PowerShell
  write; question marks are now zero and the CJK count went back up.

WHAT WAS PLANNED IN EACH NEW WORKSPACE
  deliverables\ logs\ scripts\ workspace\ data\ archive\   the six category folders (global rule 1)
  reference_file\                                          the user's drop folder (agent read-only)
  tools\New-ConversationWorkspace.ps1                      the one workspace-agnostic tool
  AGENTS.md                                                local rules: inherits the global layer, states the
                                                           workspace path, gives the command to run, and
                                                           points at E:\Deepseek as the authority
  README.md                                                index, commands and a source table

DELIBERATE DESIGN DECISION: NO policy\ COPY
  The rule set lives once, in E:\Deepseek\policy. Copying it into every workspace would create N
  divergent copies - the exact failure mode the single-source design exists to prevent. The new
  workspaces therefore inherit the global injected layer and REFERENCE the authority, and their
  AGENTS.md says explicitly not to maintain a local rule copy. Test-StoragePolicy.ps1 and
  Backup-Rules.ps1 were consequently NOT copied, because both are bound to a workspace that actually
  owns a policy (they would report a missing policy file elsewhere).

VERIFICATION
  Tool syntax OK and UTF-8 BOM intact in both copies; category folders and both markdown files present;
  the run test above proves the root inference.
  Backup refreshed per rule 8 because tools\ changed.

TWO PRE-EXISTING WARNINGS IN E:\deepseek, LOOKED AT, NOT TOUCHED
  1. eim_config.toml sits in the workspace root, outside the allow list. It is an ESP-IDF installer
     (EIM) configuration for ESP32-S3 / IDF v5.3.6, i.e. the user's tooling, not agent output. Left in
     place; the user should decide whether to allow-list it or move it (EIM expects it in the project
     root, so allow-listing may be the right answer).
  2. Non-ASCII file names under workspace\ (wind-sensor manual, 硬件连接.txt, "I2C - 副本.OBJ", ...).
     These are user materials from another conversation, not agent output, so nothing was moved. The
     rule-compliant destination would be reference_file\, which exempts user files from the ASCII rule.
  Neither is a defect in the rules themselves; both are placement issues in user-owned material.

